WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:01.902
- Roughly every year,
(pleasant piano music)

00:00:01.902 --> 00:00:06.069
360,000 tons of fishing
gear is lost in the water.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.491
Even though they're abandoned,

00:00:09.491 --> 00:00:11.964
they are still catching animals,
and I was always appalled

00:00:11.964 --> 00:00:14.524
that nobody was doing anything about 'em.

00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:18.357
- [Man] There is a lot
of good organisms here.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.535
(reflective piano music)

00:00:21.535 --> 00:00:22.439
- My name is Kurt Lieber.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:25.155
I'm the President and Founder
of Ocean Defenders Alliance.

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:26.863
We get volunteer divers
(splashing)

00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:29.099
to remove abandoned
commercial fishing gear.

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:31.694
So all the nets, traps, and
lines that are out there

00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:33.053
that are still killing animals,

00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:35.386
that's what we focus on removing.

00:00:35.386 --> 00:00:37.772
Typically, in a year, we will have 10

00:00:37.772 --> 00:00:40.454
to 12 whale entanglements
from this fishing gear.

00:00:40.454 --> 00:00:43.240
Over the last four years,
that number has climbed

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.157
from 12, roughly, to 72,
so it's getting worse.

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:49.866
(moves into curious bouncy music)

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:52.028
Our main focus are
abandoned lobster traps.

00:00:52.028 --> 00:00:54.414
These lobster traps and crab traps

00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:56.608
have lines attached to 'em, and the line

00:00:56.608 --> 00:00:58.855
has a surface marker buoy
that goes to the surface.

00:00:58.855 --> 00:01:01.343
And a boat comes along and cuts that,

00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:04.003
or it gets moved around in the storms

00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:05.933
and the fishermen can't find it.

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.402
They don't leave 'em behind
just for no good reason.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.653
- [Man] Be prepared to move
fast if I need to push us off.

00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:16.676
- Our expeditions are pretty
chaotic, I'd have to say.

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:21.121
We start out early in the
morning with, usually six divers.

00:01:21.121 --> 00:01:23.600
It's really difficult
to locate these traps,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.899
'cause obviously you can't
see through the water.

00:01:25.899 --> 00:01:28.546
It's like a hunt and peck kind of a thing,

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:30.753
and it's not cost effective.

00:01:30.753 --> 00:01:32.477
But when you're talkin' about life,

00:01:32.477 --> 00:01:34.881
does it matter if it's
cost effective or not?

00:01:34.881 --> 00:01:36.018
Not in my eyes.

00:01:36.018 --> 00:01:38.544
I get 'em out to a site
and we drop anchor.

00:01:38.544 --> 00:01:39.785
(splashing)

00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:41.178
Say they come across a trap.

00:01:41.178 --> 00:01:42.867
They attach a lift bag to it.

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:44.806
The lift bag then goes to the surface.

00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:47.458
- [Man] Comin' up to the surface.

00:01:47.458 --> 00:01:49.147
- With the trap attached to it,

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:50.775
and then we put it on the hoist.

00:01:50.775 --> 00:01:51.608
- [Man] Clear!

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:53.066
(murmuring)

00:01:53.066 --> 00:01:54.271
(moves into energetic orchestral music)

00:01:54.271 --> 00:01:57.481
- The oceans have been really
decimated over our lifetime.

00:01:57.481 --> 00:01:58.587
I'm a fighter.

00:01:58.587 --> 00:01:59.824
I'll go down fighting,

00:01:59.824 --> 00:02:03.043
and most of my volunteers
feel the same way.

00:02:03.043 --> 00:02:05.188
I don't know if I'm
making a big difference

00:02:05.188 --> 00:02:08.549
in the overall scope
of worldwide problems.

00:02:08.549 --> 00:02:10.897
You can only tackle so much in your life,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.408
and I'm doin' what I can personally.

00:02:13.408 --> 00:02:15.271
When you get down there and see the impact

00:02:15.271 --> 00:02:19.438
that you're making locally,
that's what keeps us going.

00:02:25.258 --> 00:02:28.341
(echoing sonic ding)

